---------------------------------------------------------------\nBagelVLA:インターリーブされたビジョン-言語-アクション生成を介した長い水平線操作の強化〖JST機械翻訳〗 | 文献情報 | J-GLOBAL 科学技術総合リンクセンター (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202602211661631478)\nciteturn31638search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\nプレプリント\n\nJ-GLOBAL ID：202602211661631478   整理番号：26P0042049\n\n# BagelVLA:インターリーブされたビジョン-言語-アクション生成を介した長い水平線操作の強化〖JST機械翻訳〗\n\nBagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\n  * 出版者サイト {{ this.onShowPLink() }} 複写サービスで全文入手\n  * このテーマを更に深掘りする（JDreamⅢへ）\n\nこの文献はプレプリントです。プレプリントについてはこちらをご確認ください。\n\narXiv掲載論文の撤回有無については、一次情報をご確認下さい。\n\n著者 (12件)：\n\nHu Yucheng\n\nHu Yucheng について\n\n  * 名寄せID(JGPN) 202450001076978520 ですべてを検索\n  * 「Hu Yucheng」ですべてを検索\n\n, \n\nZhang Jianke\n\nZhang Jianke について\n\n  * 名寄せID(JGPN) 202450001077865169 ですべてを検索\n  * 「Zhang Jianke」ですべてを検索\n\n, \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/bagelvla-enhancing-long-horizon-manipulation-via-interleaved-vision-language/wrk_d62e047dcbec08b484e8c41af66f5409)\nciteturn31638search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; # BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\n# BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\nYucheng Hu, Jianke Zhang, Yuanfei Luo, Yanjiang Guo, Xiaoyu Chen, Xinshu Sun, Kun Feng, Qingzhou Lu, Sheng Chen, Yangang Zhang, Wei Li, Jianyu Chen\n\narXiv 2026\n\n## TL;DR\n\nBagelVLA is a unified Vision-Language-Action model that seamlessly interleaves linguistic planning, visual forecasting, and action generation within a single transformer architecture. By introducing Residual Flow Guidance (RFG), the model extracts predictive visual features via single-step denoising, enabling robust, real-time control for complex, long-horizon robotic manipulation tasks.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nBuilding a generalist robot requires an embodied agent to master three core capabilities: understanding instructions (linguistic reasoning), predicting physical outcomes (visual forecasting), and executing precise movements (action generation).\n\nWhile modern Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have made significant strides, they typically treat these capabilities in isolation. Some models excel at high-level semantic planning but lack a physical understanding of the scene's dynamics. Others use video diffusion models to predict future frames but struggle with the logical reasoning required for multi-stage tasks. BagelVLA bridges this gap by unifying reasoning, prediction, and control into a single interleaved execution loop, preventing the performance drops typically seen in complex, long-horizon manipulation tasks.\n\n## Methods\n\nBagelVLA utilizes a Mixture of Transformers (MoT) architecture initialized from Bagel, a pretrained unified multimodal understanding and generation model. The framework orchestrates three specialized experts connected via self-attention mechanisms:\n\n  1. Understanding Expert (LLM): A 7B Qwen2.5-based model that autoregressively generates textual subtasks ($l_t$) from global instructions ($L$) and visual observations ($vt$).\n  2. Generation Expert: A 7B model paired with a FLUX VAE that acts as a world model, predicting future keyframes ($v_{t+k}$) via Flow Matching.\n  3. Action Expert: A compact 2B transformer that processes proprioceptive states and outputs continuous action chunks ($a_t$).\nImage: Illustration of the BagelVLA framework showing the interleaved sequence of subtask text, keyframe image, and action tokens.Expand\n\nExtracted figure from page 2 of BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\n### Residual Flow Guidance (RFG)\n\nA major bottleneck in combining visual generation with real-time control is inference latency; standard video diffusion models require dozens of denoising steps. To solve this, the authors introduce Residual Flow Guidance (RFG). Instead of generating future frames from random noise, RFG uses the current observation as a strong structural prior ($v_{t+k}^{\\tau=0} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(v_t, I)$). It performs a single-step denoising operation to capture the residual changes toward the next keyframe, drastically reducing computational overhead while retaining predictive visual features to guide the action expert.\n\nThe model is trained using a two-stage strategy:\n\n  * Stage 1 (Pretraining): Jointly trains the understanding and generation experts on massive multimodal and action-free robotic datasets to build strong planning and world-modeling priors.\n  * Stage 2 (Finetuning): Introduces the action expert and fine-tunes the entire network on downstream robot datasets with action labels.\n\n## Datasets\n\nBagelVLA leverages a massive hybrid dataset split across its training stages:\n\n  * Pretraining Data: Includes 2.98M General VQA pairs (LLaVA-Pretrain, FineVision), 310k episodes of egocentric human videos (Egodex), and over 500k episodes of open-source and self-collected robotic data (AgibotWorld, GR, Galaxea, Bridge, Robotwin, ALOHA).\n  * Downstream Benchmarks: Evaluated on the simulated Calvin (ABC-D split) and Robotwin 2.0 environments, alongside real-world evaluations using an AgileX dual-arm robot system across basic and long-horizon task suites.\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * Thinking and Imagining Together: Forcing the model to explicitly reason through a causal chain—generating a text plan, then \"imagining\" the visual outcome, and finally acting—significantly improves success rates on long-horizon tasks compared to treating actions as a black-box mapping from observations.\n  * Prior-Driven Denoising: Full image synthesis is unnecessary for control. By shifting the diffusion starting point from pure Gaussian noise to the current frame, the generation expert can focus entirely on dynamic manipulation changes rather than reconstructing static backgrounds.\nImage: Visual comparison showing that RFG preserves backgrounds and generates high-quality future frames in far fewer steps than naive single-step denoising.Expand\n\nExtracted figure from page 2 of BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Simulation Benchmarks: On the Calvin ABC-D benchmark, BagelVLA achieved an average completion length of 4.405, outperforming competitive baselines like π0 (3.648) and VPP (4.329). On Robotwin, it achieved a 75.26% success rate in clean settings, a massive leap over π0's 46.42%.\n  * Real-World Performance: In multi-task real-world evaluations, BagelVLA achieved a 75.5% average success rate across 9 basic skills, demonstrating strong out-of-distribution generalization on unseen objects.\n  * Long-Horizon Tasks: On complex tasks like stacking cubes in a requested order or calculating and placing symbol blocks, BagelVLA outperformed baselines by a wide margin, achieving up to 95% planning accuracy.\n  * Inference Speed: Thanks to the single-step RFG mechanism, BagelVLA achieves an inference latency of 1.23 seconds per chunk on a single GPU, translating to a real-world action frequency of 40Hz (and up to 72Hz using asynchronous execution).\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\nThe authors note a distinct gap between the model's high planning accuracy (~90% correct semantic motion trends) and its final task success rate. This discrepancy suggests that while the high-level multimodal reasoning loop is highly effective, limitations still exist in the precision of low-level fine-motor control and action mapping. Future work will likely focus on improving the action expert's dexterity and control resolution.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Yuanfei Luo (https://dblp.org/pid/169/3379)\nciteturn31638search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation.\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-09849\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2602-09849\n\nYucheng Hu, Jianke Zhang, Yuanfei Luo, Yanjiang Guo, Xiaoyu Chen, Xinshu Sun, Kun Feng, Qingzhou Lu, Sheng Chen, Yangang Zhang, Wei Li, Jianyu Chen:\nBagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation. CoRR abs/2602.09849 (2026)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i5]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-09972\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/bagelvla-enhancing-long-horizon-manipulation-via-interleaved-vision-language-action-generation-517-e4c743d3)\nciteturn31638search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: last week; Back ArXiv cs.RO BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\nBack ArXiv cs.RO BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\nArXiv cs.RO\n\n# BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation\n\nJianke Zhang, Jianyu Chen, Kun Feng, Qingzhou Lu, Sheng Chen, Wei Li, Xiaoyu Chen, Xinshu Sun +4 more\n\n2602.09849 Published Feb 10, 2026\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Feb 10, 2026\n\nUpdated: Feb 11, 2026\n\nCategories: cs.RO\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nEquipping embodied agents with the ability to reason about tasks, foresee physical outcomes, and generate precise actions is essential for general-purpose manipulation. While recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have leveraged pre-trained foundation models, they typically focus on either linguistic planning or visual forecasting in isolation. These methods rarely integrate both capabilities simultaneously to guide action generation, leading to suboptimal performance in complex, long-horizon manipulation tasks. To bridge this gap, we propose BagelVLA, a unified model that integrates linguistic planning, visual forecasting, and action generation within a single framework. Initialized from a pretrained unified understanding and generative model, BagelVLA is trained to interleave textual reasoning and visual prediction directly into the action execution loop. To efficiently couple these modalities, we introduce Residual Flow Guidance (RFG), which initializes from current observation and leverages single-step denoising to extract predictive visual features, guiding action generation with minimal latency. Extensive experiments demonstrate that BagelVLA outperforms existing baselines by a significant margin on multiple simulated and real-world benchmarks, particularly in tasks requiring multi-stage reasoning.\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 