\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-12063\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHydra-Nav: Object Navigation via Adaptive Dual-Process Reasoning — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/hydra-nav-object-navigation-via-adaptive-dual-process-reasoning/wrk_f0aa277c8869537eae734b70bbab7e38)\nciteturn31638search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Hydra-Nav: Object Navigation via Adaptive Dual-Process Reasoning ... Object goal navigation—the task of finding a specific object like a \"sofa\" in an unseen environment—is a foundational challenge in embodied AI.\n\n# Hydra-Nav: Object Navigation via Adaptive Dual-Process Reasoning\n\nZixuan Wang, Huang Fang, Shaoan Wang, Yuanfei Luo, Heng Dong, Wei Li, Yiming Gan\n\nICML 2026 · OpenReview\n\n## Introduction: The Efficiency-Effectiveness Trap in Embodied AI\n\nObject goal navigation—the task of finding a specific object like a \"sofa\" in an unseen environment—is a foundational challenge in embodied AI. While Large Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have introduced impressive common-sense reasoning to this domain, they often fall into an \"efficiency-effectiveness\" trap.\n\nCurrent VLM-based agents generally split into two extremes:\n\n  1. Ineffective Agents: These rely on simple reactive policies. Lacking deep spatial reasoning, they frequently get stuck in loops or fail to navigate complex layouts.\n  2. Inefficient Agents: These employ \"Chain-of-Thought\" (CoT) reasoning at every single step. While this boosts success rates, it is computationally ruinous, causing the robot to freeze for seconds before making even minor movements.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHeng Dong | AI Researcher (https://drdh.cc/)\nciteturn31638search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Hydra-Nav: Object Navigation via Adaptive Dual-Process Reasoning\n\nPDF Code BibTex\n\n  3. Your browser does not support the video tag. ICML 2026\n\nHydra-Nav: Object Navigation via Adaptive Dual-Process Reasoning\n\nZixuan Wang, Huang Fang, Shaoan Wang, Yuanfei Luo, Heng Dong, Wei Li, Yiming Gan\n\nTL;DR Hydra-Nav is a VLM-based navigation agent that achieves state-of-the-art performance by adaptively switching between a slow reasoning mode for high-level planning and a fast reactive mode for efficient execution.\n\nPDF Project Page BibTex\n\n  4. Image ACL 2026\n\nROMA: Real-time Omni-Multimodal Assistant with Interactive Streaming Understanding\n\nXueyun Tian, Wei Li, Bingbing Xu, Heng Dong, Yuanzhuo Wang, Huawei Shen\n\nTL;DR ROMA is a real-time omni-multimodal assistant that unifies reactive answering and proactive interaction (such as alerts and narration) in streaming audio-video environments by utilizing synchronized multimodal units and a decoupled speak head for precise timing control.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Yangang Zhang (https://dblp.org/pid/41/5592.html)\nciteturn31638search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; BagelVLA: Enhancing Long-Horizon Manipulation via Interleaved Vision-Language-Action Generation.\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-09849\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npe